And there is a situation with some pedestrians
and vehicles where the circumstances seem

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.360
to be the same.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.930
But there's something we call human judgment
that is applied, which sometimes gets each

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:02.510
individual to make a different decision, whether
it's driving, whether it's about a diagnostic

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.190
test that a doctor wants to do in in your
world.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:12.290
How do you start quantifying this X factor
of human judgment and thinking about it in

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:15.760
a way that we can start making some observations
about it?

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:21.860
Speaker 5: Let's first start by double clicking
on what you call human judgment here, because

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.680
in the examples you've taken, yes, we have
human judgment in some cases.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.040
We also have human judgment, but we don't
mind variation.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:37.400
So say we are deciding, you know, what what
hotel we prefer in a foreign city or what

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.439
dish we prefer in the menu.

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:41.510
It's fine if you and I disagree.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.190
That's not something where we where we expect
uniformity.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:50.600
The problem that we've been writing about
in lawyers is when we have human judgments

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.049
on which we expect uniformity, on which we
think there is a correct answer.

00:01:55.049 --> 00:02:00.130
For instance, as you said, when the patient
is visiting a doctor, the diagnosis should

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.270
not be a matter of opinion.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.260
It should not be a matter of taste or preference.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.079
It should be a matter of professional judgment.

00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:11.030
And we pretty much expect that different doctors
would come to the same judgment.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.310
And the problem is quite often they don't.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:18.510
And that's what we call noise, the unwanted
variability in human judgments.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:24.500
Speaker 1: What are the factors that we what
are the categories of factors that can create

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.880
these differences?

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.030
You know, I remember reading about in fact,
it may have been a Daniel Kahneman book.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:36.650
Now, I don't remember offhand about sentencing
in criminal cases and how one factor that

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.769
affected sentencing was, is it close to lunchtime
or is it after lunchtime?

00:02:41.769 --> 00:02:47.970
In other words, how hungry is the judge seemed
to play a role in determining a sentence?

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:55.670
What what category would that type of of a
judgment discrepancy fall under and what types

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:56.900
of categories are there?

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:02.140
Speaker 5: So this category, the example you
take here, which is a famous study of judicial

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.310
decisions, is what we call occasion noize
occasion.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:11.989
Noise is the fact that the same people looking
at the same facts on two different occasions

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:14.790
will sometimes not have exactly the same judgment.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:20.370
So a judge looking at the same defendants
tried for the same case before lunch or after

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.291
lunch will not have exactly the same judgment.

00:03:22.291 --> 00:03:28.330
And by the way, this is true also of factors
such as the temperature, whether the weather

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:33.930
is sunny or rainy, whether the judge's favorite
football team won or lost the game the previous

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:40.140
day, and whether the previous cases that the
judge looked at were favorable or unfavorable.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.760
So there are a lot of factors that are going
to influence the judge that are completely

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.760
extraneous to the case that should have nothing
to do with the case but influence it.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.530
That's what we call occasion noise.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.439
It's variation within the person.

00:03:53.439 --> 00:03:59.500
And we can all feel this when we when we know,
oh, I made that decision because I was in

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:00.500
a bad mood.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.530
If I think back on it, I would have made a
different decision.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.569
Now that I'm in a good mood, that's that's
fairly intuitive.

00:04:06.569 --> 00:04:07.569
We're aware of that.

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:10.320
Who probably underestimates it, but we're
aware of it.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.410
That's one of the categories.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:14.560
Speaker 1: Can you give us some of the other
other categories?

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:15.560
Speaker 5: Sure.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:20.650
So another category which is relatively easy
to wrap your mind around is if you take again

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:25.700
the example of judges that people have different
average levels of severity.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:31.060
So some judges are going to be tough hanging
judges, other judges are going to be more

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.060
lenient.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.100
They're, you know, the bleeding hard judges
who feel for the defendant.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:41.130
If you give these two judges the same 20 different
cases, of course, each of the twenty cases

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.130
is different.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:46.280
But the average sentence that the bleeding
heart judge gives is going to be a lot less

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:47.440
than the average sentence.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.259
And the judge gives.

00:04:49.259 --> 00:04:51.530
That's what we call level noise.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:55.800
And then there is a third category, which
is a bit harder to wrap your mind around,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:59.520
but which is actually the most important one.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.520
And it's easy to to see if you think again,
if the judges take these two judges, the lenient

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:11.860
one and the and the tough one is not only
do they disagree on the average level of their

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.160
sentences, they're also going to disagree
on each case.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:22.500
If you ask them to rank the cases from the
the worst case to the least civilly punishable

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:28.580
case, they are not going to agree on the ranking
because basically they have different tastes.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:30.520
They are different individuals.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.070
They have different life experiences.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:33.860
They have different preferences.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.340
They may have different political opinions.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:41.250
Basically, they see different things in the
cases that they look at.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:45.080
They have different patterns of judgment and
that's what we call pattern lawyers.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:46.140
So we have occasion.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.979
Those we have level knows we have patronized.

00:05:48.979 --> 00:05:53.800
When you put all those things together, you
have a lot of reasons for judgments that should

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:59.050
be identical, ideally to, in fact, differ
and differ quite a bit.

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:05.949
Speaker 1: So there are people who are maybe
technocratic by nature who when you present

00:06:05.949 --> 00:06:11.620
this to them, they would come up with a solution,
maybe when it comes to the sentencing question,

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:17.460
or maybe when it comes to when a patient comes
in with certain complaints, what's the path

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.940
of testing that that should be done where
they would essentially say we need to eliminate

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:29.760
some of this variability, we will use a computer
program of some kind, artificial intelligence.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:35.650
We put in the inputs and it tells us this
person should get seven years in prison and

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:40.370
everybody gets seven years in prison or this
patient should get the CAT scan.

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:41.370
This one should not.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:43.030
That would be one approach to this.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:51.710
Is that does that account for the true nature
of complexity with many of these situations,

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:54.870
or would that solution leave something out
that's important?

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:58.430
Speaker 5: Well, there is a difficult tradeoff
to make here.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:02.650
What is guaranteed is that if you do what
you just described, you will eliminate the

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:03.650
noise.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:08.350
Yes, because whatever system, whatever rule,
whatever algorithm, whatever formula you put

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:10.669
in place is going to be noise free.

00:07:10.669 --> 00:07:16.680
And that's why it's often when you actually
measure the performance of algorithms and

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:21.720
rules and so on, it's often quite good because
it eliminates the noise, the level noise,

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.289
the occasional is the better noise.

00:07:23.289 --> 00:07:27.590
It basically makes sure that when you have
the same inputs, you will have the same output.

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:33.200
Now, does that make does that necessarily
make rules and algorithms a good thing?

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.090
No, because noise is not the only way you
can be wrong.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.690
You can also have bias.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:44.270
So suppose to take an extreme but not completely
crazy case that you have a rule that says

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:50.889
anyone who's been sentenced of this particular
crime gets exactly this punishment, or anyone

00:07:50.889 --> 00:07:55.270
who's been sentenced three times of a felony
goes to jail for life.

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.020
Three three strikes and you're out.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:58.020
Yes.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:01.020
Well, you've eliminated noise there, at least
if you apply the rule.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.060
But a lot of people would say you've created
another form of error, which is a form of

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:08.150
bias, which is that you are.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:13.169
Giving too severe a sentence to a lot of people
who would not deserve that sentence.

00:08:13.169 --> 00:08:17.789
Yes, you've eliminated the unwanted variability
between people who have committed the same

00:08:17.789 --> 00:08:23.490
three crimes, but you've added a lot of bias
in the form of unwanted severity against people

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:27.199
who have committed three not so bad crimes.

00:08:27.199 --> 00:08:33.519
So there's a trade off between how accurate
and how unbiased an algorithm or a rule can

00:08:33.519 --> 00:08:38.479
be and the fact that it will be noise free
because that tradeoff is a difficult one.

00:08:38.479 --> 00:08:42.849
There is going to be lots of cases where,
in fact, you will have to continue to resort

00:08:42.849 --> 00:08:43.849
to human judgment.

00:08:43.849 --> 00:08:49.080
You cannot replace it with a rule and therefore
you need to try to make it better, less noisy

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:55.570
without eliminating the human component
Speaker 1: where where biases affect human

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:56.570
judgment.

00:08:56.570 --> 00:09:03.020
Sometimes it has been argued that being aware
of these biases can help mitigate their impact.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:08.730
So whether it's availability bias or whether
it's confirmation bias, that understanding

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:13.769
that this is something that might impact our
judgment helps us to reduce it when it comes

00:09:13.769 --> 00:09:21.529
to noise, is is it is awareness also useful
in mitigating it in an individual or is it

00:09:21.529 --> 00:09:22.529
less useful?

00:09:22.529 --> 00:09:26.870
Speaker 5: So first of all, for bias, there
isn't actually a lot of evidence that simply

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:34.149
being aware of the biases is an effective,
lasting, sustainable way to reduce your biases.

00:09:34.149 --> 00:09:35.950
It doesn't work that well.

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:36.950
Awareness is good.

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:42.110
It makes you more careful, but it's not in
itself sufficient for noise.

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:43.110
It's even worse.

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:45.490
OK, because here's the problem of noise.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:48.380
You don't know which way it's going to make
you go wrong.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:52.730
You don't know of all the possible sources
of noise, of everything that affects you in

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:57.260
a particular occasion or for your individual
preferences that could be different from somebody

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:58.260
else's.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:02.330
Of all the factors that can influence you
on a particular case, you don't know in what

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:04.340
direction it's going to make you go wrong.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:09.410
So you need to actually take a stance that
we call decision hygene.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:15.110
That is a form of prevention against noise
without knowing how noise is going to affect

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:16.190
you.

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:22.040
The reason we call it decision hygiene, which
is slightly off-putting and that's intentional,

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:26.690
is that we we liken this to washing your hands.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:31.269
When you wash your hands, you don't know what
germs you're eliminating.

00:10:31.269 --> 00:10:35.000
You you're not saying, oh, this is the covid
virus and this is the flu virus.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.350
And if you have these two particular things
and in fact, if you're successful, you will

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:41.440
never know what problem you've prevented.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:45.899
But you know that it's good discipline, that
it's good practice, that it's good hygiene.

00:10:45.899 --> 00:10:51.649
Likewise, everything that you do in your decision
making process, ahead of the decision, ahead

00:10:51.649 --> 00:10:57.030
of time to make your decision process more
disciplined, is going to improve the quality

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:59.270
of your decisions because it's going to make
you less noisy

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:03.880
Speaker 1: when it comes to institutions versus
individuals.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:09.720
Noise at the institutional level sometimes
can lead to what what some call systemic types

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:10.740
of biases.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:17.760
You could imagine how noise and human judgment
might lead to racial discrimination in housing

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:22.970
decisions, for example, or transportation
decisions about where should the stops for

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:28.029
the subway line be, which could be influenced
by all of these types of factors.

00:11:28.029 --> 00:11:34.899
Do you approach institutional decision making
noise differently than, for example, a doctor

00:11:34.899 --> 00:11:39.170
or a driver making a decision about an individual
patient, for example?

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:42.720
Speaker 5: We actually view the doctor as
part of an institution.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:47.080
When you only see one doctor, you will never
know if your doctor is noisy.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:51.260
You go to a doctor, your doctor tells you
you have the flu, you go home, you take your

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:53.520
medicine and it gets better or it doesn't
get better.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:57.430
You will never know whether another doctor
in the same hospital would have told you,

00:11:57.430 --> 00:11:58.490
no, it's not the flu, it's covid-19.

00:11:58.490 --> 00:11:59.490
Right.

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:00.490
Or it's not the flu.

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:02.000
It's something else.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:08.330
So if you think about who should take care
of noise, it's actually always the institution.

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:11.810
As individuals, we can try to make ourselves
more disciplined.

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:19.279
But the institution, whether it's the hospital
or the justice system, the the organization

00:12:19.279 --> 00:12:24.959
that takes care of hiring individuals or the
company that has a hiring process, all those

00:12:24.959 --> 00:12:31.020
institutions should try to reduce both the
noise and the biases in their decisions.

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:35.930
And if they put in place good decision hiring
practices, they will reduce noise.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:40.430
They will also at the same time reduce the
sorts of systemic biases that you are talking

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:44.660
about, because when you put in place good
decision practices, you will make your decisions

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:49.910
less exposed, less dependance, less sensitive
to the biases of individuals.

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:54.380
Speaker 1: The last thing I wanted to talk
about is whether there are some cases where

00:12:54.380 --> 00:13:00.260
we're maybe both noise and bias are good in
the sense that they are sort of what makes

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:05.990
us uniquely human in the example that I will
give is as we learn more about autonomous

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:07.360
vehicles and the.

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:13.209
Decisions that drivers are sometimes making
