00:15:40.269 --> 00:15:42.500
human judgment, but how to replace it.

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:46.589
There will be lots of decisions that we will
want humans to make, either because it's too

00:15:46.589 --> 00:15:52.130
difficult to have a machine that makes it
or because we don't want the machine to make

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:57.640
it in our place, because we we care about
dealing with the different human being.

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.480
We want to give people a sense of agency.

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:04.009
We want to give people who interact with the
decision the dignity of dealing with the human

00:16:04.009 --> 00:16:05.170
being and not with the machine.

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:10.100
There are lots of very valid reasons to retain
human judgment.

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:13.970
And when that is the case, we need to make
sure that those judgments, if they cannot

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:18.589
be the sort of expert snap second judgments
that you are talking about, that they are

00:16:18.589 --> 00:16:23.860
made in a considered careful way so that they
are less noisy, more just and less prone to

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:24.860
error.

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:28.070
Speaker 1: That that that makes a lot of sense.

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:33.490
We've been speaking about the book Noise A
Flaw in Human Judgment, co-written by Daniel

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:39.180
Kahneman, Cass Sunstein and our guest today,
Professor Olivia Khubani.

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:40.240
So great having you on.

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:41.720
I really appreciate your time today.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:42.209
Speaker 5: Thank you very much, David

